Regression test for the sockbplog reply-buffer use-after-free.

A replicant ships its bplog to the master over a "sockbplog" appsock and then
waits for the commit rc on that same socket.  On the master, the block
processor writes that rc back over the appsock's own buffer (sess->target->sb),
from a different thread than the appsock handler.

The appsock used to dispatch the session and then go back to waiting for the
next request on the same connection.  When the replicant gave up and closed,
that read returned EOF, the handler returned, and the framework freed the
connection buffer -- so any reply landing afterwards wrote into freed memory.

The test forces the writer to reply long after the appsock is done, using
tunables that already exist:

  sleep_before_dispatch (master)       stalls the block processor before it
                                       runs the transaction, so the reply is
                                       written STALL seconds late.

  SOSQL_MAX_COMMIT_WAIT_SEC (lrl)      how long the replicant waits for that
                                       rc.  Set well below STALL so the
                                       replicant has certainly given up, and
                                       closed its end of the sockbplog socket,
                                       before the reply is written.

  survive_n_master_swings (lrl)        set to 0.  The commit-wait timeout is
                                       reported as ERR_NOMASTER, which the
                                       replicant otherwise retries up to 600
                                       times.  Since the client cannot succeed
                                       while the master is stalled, that is an
                                       unbounded retry storm, and each attempt
                                       parks another master write thread for
                                       STALL seconds until the write pool is
                                       exhausted and the cluster wedges.  With
                                       0 the timeout is terminal and each
                                       client abandons exactly one transaction.

  DISABLE_SERVER_SOCKPOOL (lrl)        so the replicant's disconnect really
                                       closes the socket instead of donating it
                                       back to the pool.  We want the master's
                                       appsock to see EOF and free its buffer,
                                       which is the condition under test.

Without the fix the master writes the reply into a buffer the appsock has
already freed.  That is heap corruption, so it can surface as a core (which the
harness picks up), as a hang, or as damage that only shows up on later traffic
-- which is why the test also replays a clean, verifiable workload afterwards
and checks the cluster is still healthy.

With the fix the buffer is refcounted (struct bplog_sock, db/osqlsqlsocket.c).
The appsock sets *arg->keepsocket so the framework never closes the buffer, and
holds one reference for the connection; each session it dispatches holds another,
taken in init_bplog_socket_master() and dropped in osql_sess_close().  Whichever
side finishes last calls close_appsock().  Neither side waits for the other: the
appsock returns as soon as it sees the connection drop, and the block processor
is never held up by the appsock.

That last property is the one this test cannot see, and it is worth knowing why.
DISABLE_SERVER_SOCKPOOL is what blinds it: with the pool off the replicant's
disconnect is a real close, so the master's appsock gets EOF at once.  Make the
writer wait on the appsock -- as an earlier version of this fix did, by having
osql_sess_close() block until the appsock released the session's client ref --
and with sockpool ON there is no EOF at all, so every transaction parks a write
thread for the full appsock read timeout (IOTIMEOUTMS, 10s).  At maxwt 8 that is
0.8 txn/s and the cluster is effectively wedged.  This test drives only 8
transactions and turns sockpool off, so it sails through either way;
bplog_leaks (sockbplog) is what caught it, by timing out.

Also not covered here: an fd/appsock leak if a reference is ever dropped twice or
not at all.  Nothing in this test would notice, and it is the natural failure
mode of shared-ownership bugs, so it is worth checking by hand when this code
changes -- active_appsock_conns should return to its baseline after the storm.
